WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.596
"Boosting Heart Nerve Control"

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:07.242
Next, everyone feel for your pulse.

00:00:07.242 --> 00:00:09.283
Stick your hand out, thumbs up,

00:00:09.283 --> 00:00:13.022
feel for the knobby bone
at the top of your wrist.

00:00:13.022 --> 00:00:17.250
Then, if you slide your hand down
across the front of your wrist,

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.981
you’ll feel these like
strands of spaghetti—

00:00:19.981 --> 00:00:23.048
those are the tendons in your wrist.

00:00:23.048 --> 00:00:28.898
If you lightly feel between the
knobby bone and that first tendon,

00:00:28.898 --> 00:00:32.174
you should feel your pulse.

00:00:32.174 --> 00:00:33.743
Got it?

00:00:33.743 --> 00:00:36.102
Okay, now we’re going
to feel what happens

00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:39.872
to our pulse when we take a deep breath.

00:00:39.872 --> 00:00:41.523
We’re going to try it twice.

00:00:41.523 --> 00:00:45.683
Try to feel what happens to
your pulse when you inhale,

00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:47.874
and when you exhale.

00:00:47.874 --> 00:00:48.892
All right, here we go—

00:00:48.892 --> 00:00:52.801
slow inhale…

00:00:52.801 --> 00:00:58.804
slow exhale.

00:00:58.804 --> 00:01:10.521
One more time…

00:01:10.521 --> 00:01:15.083
What happened when you
breathed in and breathed out?

00:01:15.083 --> 00:01:19.871
You should have felt your pulse
speed up when you breathe in

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:23.620
and slow down when you breathe out --

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.882
that’s called heart rate variability,

00:01:26.882 --> 00:01:29.121
and it’s a very good thing.

00:01:29.121 --> 00:01:35.166
That’s a measure of the control
our nerves have over our heart.

00:01:35.166 --> 00:01:39.223
This is the survival curve for
people after a heart attack.

00:01:39.223 --> 00:01:41.622
With good heart rate variability,

00:01:41.622 --> 00:01:45.444
9 out of 10 people are
still alive four years later.

00:01:45.444 --> 00:01:50.005
But with bad heart rate variability,
in that time period,

00:01:50.005 --> 00:01:54.113
half drop dead from sudden cardiac death.

00:01:54.113 --> 00:01:55.902
Is there anything we can do

00:01:55.902 --> 00:02:01.022
to improve our neurological
control over our heart?

00:02:01.022 --> 00:02:05.703
A landmark article this year in the
American Journal of Clinical Nutrition

00:02:05.703 --> 00:02:09.561
found that there was
only one type of food

00:02:09.561 --> 00:02:14.294
that could significantly improve
our heart rate variability.

00:02:14.294 --> 00:02:17.223
Is it citrus fruits,

00:02:17.223 --> 00:02:18.813
omega-3s,

00:02:18.813 --> 00:02:22.001
or dark green leafy vegetables?

00:02:22.001 --> 00:02:24.671
Let’s look at the graph.

00:02:24.671 --> 00:02:30.172
Here’s the level at which
statistical significance is reached.

00:02:30.172 --> 00:02:34.933
Let’s see which one makes it.

00:02:34.933 --> 00:02:38.903
Eating just a half serving
of greens every day

00:02:38.903 --> 00:02:41.984
significantly improved
neuronal heart control—

00:02:41.984 --> 00:02:45.853
which may be why studies in the past

00:02:45.853 --> 00:02:48.642
have suggested a quarter
cup of greens a day

00:02:48.642 --> 00:02:53.464
could cut our risk of having
a heart attack by 67%!

